WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.470
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:09.679
This project is a collaboration between INRIA
and Facebook AI research and is about pose

00:00:09.679 --> 00:00:10.929
estimation.

00:00:10.929 --> 00:00:16.960
Pose estimation means that we take an input
photo or in the cooler case, video of people,

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.160
and the output should be a description of
their postures.

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:25.370
This is kind of like motion capture for those
amazing movie and computer game animations,

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:28.620
but without the studio and the markers.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:34.449
This work goes even further and tries to offer
a full 3D reconstruction of the geometry of

00:00:34.449 --> 00:00:39.280
the bodies, and it is in fact doing way more
than that as you will see in a minute.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:43.069
Neural networks are usually great at these
tasks, provided that we have a large number

00:00:43.069 --> 00:00:45.100
of training samples to train them.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.769
So, the first step is gathering a large amount
of annotated data.

00:00:49.769 --> 00:00:53.879
This means an input photograph of someone,
which is paired up with the correct description

00:00:53.879 --> 00:00:55.229
of their posture.

00:00:55.229 --> 00:00:57.340
This is what we call one training sample.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:03.210
This new proposed dataset contains 50 thousand
of these training samples, and using that,

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:07.980
we can proceed to step number two, training
the neural network to perform pose estimation.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.040
But, there is more to this particular work.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.660
Normally, this pose estimation takes place
with a 2D skeleton, which means that most

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.040
techniques output a stick figure.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.960
But not in this case, because the dataset
contains segmentations and dense correspondances

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:30.290
between 2D images and 3D models, therefore,
the network is also able to output fully 3D

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:31.290
models.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:35.590
There are plenty of interesting details shown
in the paper, for instance, since the annotated

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:40.250
ground truth footage in the training set is
created by humans, there is plenty of missing

00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:45.700
data that is filled in by using a separate
neural network that is specialized for this

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:46.700
task.

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.880
Make sure to have a look at the paper for
more cool details like this.

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:55.280
This all sounds good in theory, but a practical
application has to be robust against occlusions

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.700
and rapid changes in posture, and the good
thing is that the authors published plenty

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.300
of examples with these that you can see here.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:07.640
Also, it has to be able to deal with smaller
and bigger scales when people are closer or

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.500
further away from the camera.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.090
This is also a challenge.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:16.090
The algorithm does a really good job at this,
and remember, no markers and studio setup

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:21.090
is required, and everything that you see here
is performed interactively.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:26.060
The dataset will appear soon and it will be
possible to reuse it for future research works,

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:30.610
so I expect plenty of more collaboration and
followup works for this problem.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:32.810
We are living amazing times indeed.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:36.800
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

